// Volume → weight reference table for kitchen ingredients.
//
// gPerMl is grams per millilitre as the ingredient is normally measured in a cup or spoon (spooned and levelled
// flour, packed brown sugar, chopped vegetables, grated cheese …), not the solid density of the material. Values
// are derived from the King Arthur Baking ingredient weight chart (grams per US cup ÷ 236.6 ml) and USDA
// FoodData Central household measures; where the two disagree the more commonly cited value is used. They are
// approximations: volume measures of solids vary ±10 %.
//
// kind drives the unit policy (see convert.js):
//   liquid   thin liquids that stay in ml/dl/l (water, milk, cream, stock, wine, juice, spirits, extracts)
//   viscous  thick liquids weighed in g (honey, syrups, pastes, condiments)
//   fat      butter, oils, shortening (g)
//   powder   flours, starches, cocoa, powdered sugar (g)
//   granular sugars, crumbs, salts that are weighed (g)
//   spice    ground / whole spices and dried herbs (spoons when small, g otherwise)
//   leavening baking powder / soda, yeast, cream of tartar (spoons when small)
//   produce  vegetables, fruit, fresh herbs (g by volume; countables keep pieces)
//   nut      nuts, seeds, dried fruit, chocolate pieces (g)
//   grain    rice, oats, pasta, legumes, cereals (g)
//   dairy    soft / grated cheese, yogurt, sour cream (g)
//   other    eggs, meat, ice and anything else
//
// spoon: true marks ingredients that stay in metric spoons when the amount is small (≤ 1 tbsp or < 10 g) even
// though their kind is not spice or leavening (salts, extracts, gelatine powder, instant coffee, fresh herbs …).
// Any other weighable ingredient also stays in spoons without a precision scale when it is both ≤ 1 tbsp and
// under 10 g (2 tsp cornstarch, 1 tsp sugar): a 1 g kitchen scale cannot weigh that reliably.
// pieceGrams is the typical weight of one piece (edible part) for countables; recipes keep counting pieces.

